.TH CTI-MAILBOX 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-mailbox \- daily tidy of the shared CTI mailbox: archive what its owning lane has read, delete auto-replies, leave the rest
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cti-mailbox
.RB [ \-\-for\-real ]
.RB [ \-\-json
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-backlog\-threshold
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR DURATION ]
.RB [ \-\-lookback
.IR DURATION ]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-mailbox
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cti-mailbox
is the only command in the fleet that modifies the mailbox. It is run by
.BR run\-mailbox\-cleanup (1)
from
.B cti\-agent\-mailbox.timer
every day at 07:00, an hour after the digest; the unit passes
.B \-\-for\-real
explicitly.
.PP
.B Without
.B \-\-for\-real
.B nothing is moved.
The plan is computed and printed in full, and the output says
.BR "DRY RUN" .
.PP
Before looking at the mailbox it loads the processed-message logs: the
digest's, which it writes as it reads mail, and one per lane that owns mail of
its own
.RI ( state/processed\-appscan.json ,
written by
.B cti\-appscan \-\-commit\-seen
after the application-security weekly is sent). A missing log counts as
empty. A log that does not parse, or no way to locate the digest's (neither
.B FLEET_HOME
nor
.BR FLEET_PROCESSED_LOG ),
stops the run.
.PP
When no message carrying a CVE was recorded as read today (in
.BR FLEET_TIMEZONE ),
nothing is archived that day: the fleet may not be reading the mailbox.
Automatic replies are still deleted, and the fleet's own reports still
archived.
.PP
It then reads every message received in
.B GRAPH_FOLDER
within the last
.B MAILBOX_LOOKBACK_HOURS
(or
.BR \-\-lookback )
and decides each one by these rules, in this order:
.TP
.B 0. Sent by this fleet: archive
The message carries an
.B X\-CTI\-Agent\-Sent
header with a valid signature (an HMAC keyed from the app registration's
client secret, which only this fleet holds). The sender address alone is not
enough, and neither is an unsigned header. Mail signed before a client secret
rotation no longer verifies and is left for the operator. Not subject to the
archive grace.
.TP
.B 1. An automatic reply: Deleted Items, read or not
Declared by its headers
.RB ( "Auto\-Submitted: auto\-replied"
or
.BR auto\-notified ,
or
.B X\-Auto\-Response\-Suppress: All
with an
.B Auto\-Submitted
value containing
.BR auto ),
or by a subject that
.B starts
like an out-of-office:
.BR "Automatic reply:" ,
.BR "Auto-reply:" ,
.B "Out of Office:"
and their common variants (not
.BR OOO: ,
which people use for their own notices). Anyone can title a message that
way, so a subject-only match is bounded twice: one no lane has read yet waits
.B GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS
for the digest, and one the digest took a CVE from is archived instead.
Header-declared replies go on sight. Not subject to the archive grace.
.TP
.B 2. No processing record: leave
Nothing else that no lane has a record of reading is ever moved.
.TP
.B 3. Read by the lane that owns it: archive
The digest owns what it took a CVE from; the application-security lane owns
the Qualys WAS notifications its weekly reported on. Archived once the
message has been in the mailbox for
.BR MAILBOX_MIN_AGE_HOURS ;
until then it is left, with the reason stated.
.TP
.B 4. Anything else: leave
Read, but no lane owns it: a reported phish, a Defender alert, a colleague's
mail. Somebody else's job.
.PP
Every line of the plan states the action, the subject (sanitised and cut to
60 characters, because subjects are attacker-controlled) and the reason.
The summary splits the leave count into
.I never read
and
.IR "not CTI mail" ;
only the first can indicate a fault.
.PP
Nothing here can permanently delete mail. The Graph client implements
move-to-Archive and move-to-Deleted-Items and nothing else; emptying Deleted
Items is a person's job. Processing records older than 14 days are pruned by
the digest, after which a message can no longer be moved on their strength.
.SH OPTIONS
Go's flag syntax: each option may also be written with a single dash
.RB ( \-for\-real ),
and a value may follow
.B =
or a space.
.TP
.B \-\-for\-real
Actually move the messages in the plan. Archive goes to the well-known
.B archive
folder, delete to
.BR deleteditems .
Each move prints the new message ID, or
.B FAILED
with the Graph error, and a final
.B moved\~N, failed\~N, left\~N
line follows.
Default:\~\fBfalse\fR (dry run).
.TP
.BI \-\-json " FILE"
Also write the plan to
.IR FILE ,
mode 0600, as JSON:
.BR mailbox ,
.BR at ,
.B applied
(whether
.B \-\-for\-real
was given),
.BR moved ,
.BR failed ,
.B counts
and every decision. Written after the moves, so it records what happened.
A write failure is reported on standard error and does not change the exit
status. Not written when the run refuses for want of a processed message.
.TP
.BI \-\-backlog\-threshold " N"
Warn on standard error when at least
.I N
messages in the window have no processing record. A growing backlog is what
an agent that has stopped reading the mailbox looks like from outside.
Mail the agent read and left is not counted.
.B 0
or less disables the warning.
.B run\-mailbox\-cleanup
passes
.B FLEET_MAILBOX_BACKLOG_THRESHOLD
here when it is set.
Default:\~\fB25\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " DURATION"
One deadline for the whole Graph conversation, reading and every move, as a
Go duration
.RB ( 90s ,
.BR 5m ).
Default:\~\fB2m0s\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-lookback " DURATION"
Look this far back for this run only, instead of
.BR MAILBOX_LOOKBACK_HOURS ,
for a one-off sweep such as
.B \-\-lookback 720h
to clear a month of old auto-replies. Archiving still needs a processing
record, and records expire after 14 days, so a wider window mostly reaches
auto-replies. Longer windows need a longer
.BR \-\-timeout .
Default:\~\fB0\fR (use
.BR MAILBOX_LOOKBACK_HOURS ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Read from the process environment, after
.I fleet.env
is loaded (existing environment variables win).
Graph settings are in section 1 of
.IR fleet.env ,
the cleanup window in section 10, the rest under
.IR "mailbox cleanup" .
Qualys settings are not needed.
.TP
.BR TENANT_ID ", " CLIENT_ID ", " CLIENT_SECRET ", " GRAPH_MAILBOX
Required. The app registration needs
.B Mail.ReadWrite
(application, admin-consented) for the moves; without it every move fails
with 403 and the run exits 1.
.TP
.B GRAPH_FOLDER
The folder read. Default:\~\fBinbox\fR.
.TP
.B MAILBOX_LOOKBACK_HOURS
How far back to look, in whole hours. Must exceed the time between runs, and
should cover the weekly lanes' windows plus the grace, or their mail falls
out of view before it can be archived.
Default:\~\fB336\fR (14 days, the processed log's retention; 72 before 1.3.2).
.TP
.B MAILBOX_MIN_AGE_HOURS
How long a processed advisory stays in the inbox before it is archived.
Must be less than
.BR MAILBOX_LOOKBACK_HOURS ,
and, unless it is
.BR 0 ,
at least
.B GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS
so the digest has finished with a message before it is filed; the command
refuses to start otherwise.
.B 0
disables the grace and archives on sight.
Default:\~\fB48\fR.
.TP
.BR GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS ", " QUALYS_KB_MAX_AGE_HOURS
Not used by this command, but validated by the shared loader: each must be a
positive integer or the run fails. Defaults:\~\fB24\fR and \fB168\fR.
.TP
.B FLEET_PROCESSED_LOG
Path of the processed-message log.
Default:\~\fI$FLEET_HOME/state/processed\-messages.json\fR.
.TP
.B FLEET_TIMEZONE
IANA zone that decides what
.I today
means for the processed-today check. Empty or unknown: the system zone (an
unknown name is reported).
.TP
.BR FLEET_HOME ", " FLEET_ENV
Fleet layout and the
.I fleet.env
path. With neither
.B FLEET_HOME
nor
.B FLEET_PROCESSED_LOG
set the command refuses to run.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/fleet.env
Settings and the Graph client secret;
.B root:ctiagent
0640.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/processed\-messages.json
Which messages a completed digest run read, whether each carried a CVE, and
whether the sender declared it an auto-reply. 0600. The lane's entire
authority to move anything: no entry, no move. If it is corrupt, delete it;
the next digest rebuilds it and the inbox is not tidied until then.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/mailbox\-cleanup\-YYYY\-MM\-DD.json
The plan written by
.B run\-mailbox\-cleanup
through
.BR \-\-json ,
0600.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The plan was printed (dry run), or every move in it succeeded, or the run
refused because no CTI email was processed today. The refusal is deliberate:
a non-zero exit would mark the unit failed and send an alert about a lane
that behaved correctly. A backlog warning does not change the status.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration is missing or invalid; neither
.B FLEET_HOME
nor
.B FLEET_PROCESSED_LOG
is set; the processed-message log cannot be read or does not parse;
the mailbox could not be read; or at least one move failed (usually a missing
.B Mail.ReadWrite
grant).
.TP
.B 2
Unknown option or bad value (Go's flag package).
.SH EXAMPLES
Show what today's cleanup would do, moving nothing:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-mailbox
.EE
.PP
The same through the runner, which also posts the summary to the board:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-mailbox\-cleanup
.EE
.PP
Apply it, keeping the plan:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-mailbox \-\-for\-real \e
  \-\-json /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/mailbox\-cleanup\-$(date +%F).json
.EE
.PP
Check the Graph roles before the first real run:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-mailer \-\-check
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-mailbox\-cleanup (1),
.BR run\-digest (1),
.BR cti\-mailer (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
RUNBOOK.md section 10, "Rolling out mailbox cleanup".
